---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import MarginNote from '../components/MarginNote.astro';
import placeholderField from '../assets/photos/placeholder-field.jpg';
---

<Page
  label="About"
  title="Jordan Mogck"
  standfirst="I help owner-led businesses think clearly about what they’re doing, and then help them build the thing they decided on."
  description="About Jordan Mogck and the Happy Toil practice."
>
  <section class="frame section--tight section--flush">
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="col-reading">
        <div class="prose prose--with-margins">
          <p>
            Most of my work sits between two roles that businesses usually hire separately:
            the person who helps decide what to do, and the person who builds it. Keeping
            both in one head means the strategy stays honest about what’s actually
            buildable, and the software stays honest about what the business actually needs.
          </p>

          <MarginNote>
            I take on a small number of engagements at a time. When I’m full, I’ll say so
            rather than stretch.
          </MarginNote>

          <p>
            I’m most useful to businesses rooted in real work — farms, food companies,
            manufacturers, mills, trades. Operations where the product is physical, the
            margins are earned, and the owner is close enough to the work to know when an
            answer doesn’t smell right. These businesses are often underserved by
            consultants, who tend to arrive with a template, and by software companies,
            who tend to arrive with a subscription.
          </p>

          <h2>How I work</h2>

          <p>
            I start by looking. Usually that means time in the operation: the shop, the
            barn, the office, whatever spreadsheet everything actually runs on. I’d rather
            spend the first weeks understanding how money and material move through a
            business than producing a deck about it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then we argue a bit. Part of what you’re paying for is someone who will
            question the premise of the assignment. A surprising number of projects are
            solutions to a problem the business no longer has, or a symptom of one nobody
            has named yet. It’s cheaper to find that out early.
          </p>

          <p>
            After that it’s ordinary work: build the system, write the plan, fix the
            pricing, train the person who will own it once I’m gone. I try to leave
            businesses less dependent on me than when I arrived. That’s not modesty — it’s
            the only version of this that scales down honestly to one person.
          </p>

          <h2>Beyond the practice</h2>

          <p>
            Happy Toil is one vocation among several. There’s a farm, a household, family,
            and a set of long-running experiments that inform the rest of it. Working the
            land teaches you things about scale, time, and maintenance that are hard to
            learn from software: that most systems fail slowly, that the fix is usually
            upstream, and that the season doesn’t care about your roadmap.
          </p>

          <p>
            That’s the underlying idea here. Work belongs inside a life, rather than a life
            organized around work. It shapes how I think about growth, technology, profit,
            and how many clients one person should have.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="frame section--tight">
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="col-wide">
        <Figure
          src={placeholderField}
          alt="A field at the end of the day."
          number="Fig. 1"
          caption="Placeholder. Replace with a photograph of the farm, the shop, or a client operation — something that documents real work rather than illustrating it."
          width="wide"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="frame section--tight">
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="col-reading">
        <div class="prose">
          <h2>The name</h2>
          <p>
            “Happy toil” comes from Ecclesiastes, which spends twelve chapters taking apart
            every reason a person might have for working — wealth, reputation, legacy,
            being remembered — and arrives somewhere unexpected. Not that work is
            meaningless, but that a person’s portion is the work itself: the day, the meal,
            the company, the labor at hand, received as a gift rather than as leverage
            against the future.
          </p>
          <p>
            It’s an odd thing to name a consultancy after a book about the limits of
            ambition. It’s also the most accurate description I have of how I’d like to
            work, and what I’d like the businesses I work with to be able to do.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="about__invitation">
          <a class="invitation" href="/contact/"
            >Tell me about the business <span class="arrow">→</span></a
          >
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  .about__invitation {
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
  }
</style>
